   "number": 16,
   "question": "Why does the ideal fixed–free resonance series use odd multiples of the base frequency?",
   "answer": "The first pattern contains **one quarter-wave** between its still end and maximally moving end. Further patterns must add two quarter-waves at a time to preserve those different end conditions, giving one, three, five and further odd counts.",
   "teaching": [
    "An even quarter-wave count would end at the wrong kind of point. The odd sequence therefore follows from fitting the required spatial pattern.",
    "Real vowel constrictions change the tube’s shape and shift its resonances. The odd-multiple result describes the ideal neutral comparison, not a fixed ratio required of every spoken vowel."
   ],
