   "answer": "Speech coordinates complex hidden articulatory movements with little direct visual feedback or explicit instruction. **That coordination develops before the instruction-heavy skills of reading and writing**.\n\nThe movements must work together, because changing one part alters the air passage available to the others. Direct visual feedback is limited, yet the developing speaker learns a large vocabulary and increasingly adult-like coordination.",
