   "lectureId": "lecture03",
   "moduleId": "module1",
   "type": "written",
   "topic": "learning",
   "concept": "learning",
   "prompt": "Why can randomly initialized weights make a prediction before containing useful knowledge?",
   "wordings": {
    "original": "Why can randomly initialized weights make a prediction before containing useful knowledge?",
    "college": "Why can randomly initialized weights make a prediction before containing useful knowledge?",
    "simple": "Why can randomly initialized weights make a prediction before containing useful knowledge?"
   },
   "answer": "Random parameters can still be used to calculate a response. **Producing a guess does not mean the weights already encode useful knowledge**.\n\nComparing a prediction with its target reveals a mismatch. Repeated corrections change how future inputs are processed, so later responses can become more appropriate.",
