   "lectureId": "lecture03",
   "moduleId": "module1",
   "type": "written",
   "topic": "ai skills",
   "concept": "ai-skills",
   "prompt": "What can a biological comparison contribute without making a network identical to a brain?",
   "wordings": {
    "original": "What can a biological comparison contribute without making a network identical to a brain?",
    "college": "What can a biological comparison contribute without making a network identical to a brain?",
    "simple": "What can a biological comparison contribute without making a network identical to a brain?"
   },
   "answer": "It can preserve selected functional relationships while simplifying the implementation. **A useful model does not require literal biological identity**.\n\nRecognition uses current representations to identify an input, while learning changes those representations after experience. An account of knowing must connect what the system has learned to the decisions it can make.",
