 "readings": [
  [
   "notes-08-25-1",
   "Explain behavior through the system producing it.",
   [
    "**A system combines elements, relationships and a function or purpose**. Explaining its behavior requires asking how the parts influence one another, rather than treating each event as an isolated cause.",
    "A slinky’s parts have physical properties, but its behavior also depends on their connected arrangement. A systems explanation connects a continuing pattern to the organization generating it."
   ],
   "Why can listing every part fail to explain the whole system?",
   "The list leaves out **the relationships among the parts and the function of their arrangement**. Those relationships determine how one change produces another."
  ],
  [
   "notes-08-25-2",
   "Distinguish parts from organization.",
   [
    "**Elements are the parts, while interconnections describe how information or material moves among them**. A system’s purpose is reflected in its continuing behavior, which can differ from a stated intention.",
    "Replacing a part while preserving its role can leave much of the behavior intact. Changing the relationships or organizing purpose can have a more extensive effect."
   ],
   "Why can replacing an element leave the behavior much as before?",
   "The replacement can preserve **the same role, connections and organizing purpose**. Behavior depends on that organization as well as on the individual parts."
  ],
  [
   "notes-09-01",
   "Follow accumulation and corrective feedback.",
   [
    "**A stock is an accumulated quantity, and flows change it over time**. A bathtub’s level rises when inflow exceeds outflow and falls when outflow is greater.",
    "Equal flows produce **dynamic equilibrium**. The water level stays constant even while water enters and leaves, so a flat stock does not establish that nothing is happening.",
    "**Balancing feedback opposes a discrepancy from a goal**. Information about a stock affects an action that changes a flow, which then affects the stock. Changing a flow and changing an accumulated stock are different events."
   ],
   "How can a bathtub’s level remain constant while water keeps moving?",
   "**Equal inflow and outflow balance each other**. The stock stays constant while the flows remain active."
  ],
  [
   "notes-09-03",
   "Recognize a change that amplifies itself.",
   [
    "**Reinforcing feedback makes an initial change generate more change in the same direction**. A larger stock can produce a larger addition, enlarging the stock again.",
    "The same organization can amplify decline if a smaller stock produces less replenishment. Reinforcing describes the direction of influence around the loop, rather than whether the outcome is desirable. Balancing feedback instead reduces a discrepancy."
   ],
   "Why can a reinforcing loop produce decline as well as growth?",
   "It **amplifies the direction of change**. If a reduction causes further reduction, the loop reinforces decline rather than correcting it."
  ],
  [
   "notes-09-15",
   "Explain competing feedback through a heated room.",
   [
    "**A thermostat compares room temperature with a target and controls heat input**. A colder room calls for heating, and warming reduces the discrepancy. This is balancing feedback.",
    "Heat also leaks toward the colder outdoors. The room warms only when incoming heat exceeds outgoing heat, so continuing input can be necessary at a steady temperature.",
    "The controller acts through a system with losses and response time. Cooling can outweigh the available heating, explaining why a balancing loop can fail to hold its goal under changed conditions."
   ],
   "Why can a room need furnace input while its temperature stays steady?",
   "**Heat continues to escape**. A steady temperature can reflect heating that matches heat loss."
  ],
  [
   "notes-09-17",
   "Connect changing dominance with delayed correction.",
   [
    "**A stock can participate in reinforcing and balancing loops at the same time**. Births add to a population and deaths remove from it. Their relative rates determine growth, decline or equilibrium, and the dominant influence can change.",
    "Capital follows an analogous accumulation relationship through investment and depreciation. A stock can grow because of a smaller outflow as well as a larger inflow.",
    "**Delay can turn correction into repeated overshoot**. In a dealership, sales reduce inventory, perceived demand affects orders, and deliveries arrive later. Continuing to order while earlier corrections are on the way can produce a surplus followed by another correction.",
    "Orders and deliveries can swing more than the initial demand change. Aggressive correction can worsen the swings, while slower correction can damp them. A scenario explores a response to stated assumptions rather than guaranteeing a future outcome."
   ],
   "Why can a faster inventory correction produce larger oscillations?",
   "The response can grow before **delayed information and deliveries reveal the effect of earlier orders**. Continued correction overshoots the desired inventory, prompting a correction in the opposite direction."
  ]
 ],
